      "sectionName": "Sử dụng Tiếng Anh (300đ)",
      "passage": "Questions 46-52: Read the passage carefully.\nConfidence is not something people are born with — it is a skill developed through daily\nchoices and mindset shifts. One of the first steps is to stop comparing yourself to others.\nSocial media can easily make you feel inadequate, but constant comparison often leads to\nenvy and low self-esteem. Research has shown that when people measure their worth\nagainst others, their confidence decreases. The key is to focus on your own progress\ninstead of someone else’s highlight reel. Keeping a gratitude journal and reminding\nyourself of your strengths can help you shift aĴention back to what truly maĴers: your\nown journey.\nAnother crucial step in building confidence is surrounding yourself with people who\nuplift you. The company you keep can strongly influence how you feel about yourself.\nSpend time with supportive friends who celebrate your successes and accept your\nimperfections. Likewise, taking care of your physical and mental health strengthens your\nsense of self-worth. Regular exercise, nutritious food, adequate sleep, and mindfulness\npractices such as meditation all contribute to a positive self-image and emotional balance.\nWhen you treat your body with respect, you signal to yourself — and to others — that\nyou maĴer.\nSelf-compassion and positive self-talk also play a central role. Many people underestimate\nhow much inner dialogue affects their confidence. Negative thoughts like “I can’t do this”\nor “I’m not good enough” can become self-fulfilling. By reframing them into affirmations\n— “I can learn this” or “I’ll try again next time” — you strengthen your mental resilience.\nSimilarly, facing your fears instead of avoiding them helps you grow braver over time.\nEach small victory — whether it’s speaking in public, applying for a job, or trying\nsomething new — rewires your brain to believe in your own abilities.\nFinally, real confidence comes from balance. Set realistic goals instead of impossible ones,\nand know when to say no to things that drain your energy. Focusing on what you do well\nreinforces self-trust, while protecting your boundaries preserves it. Over time, confidence\nbecomes less about perfection and more about self-acceptance. Believing in yourself\ndoesn’t mean ignoring your flaws — it means acknowledging them without leĴing them\ndefine you. Confidence, at its core, is not arrogance or luck; it’s quiet assurance built\nthrough self-awareness, compassion, and consistent effort.\n(Adapted from: hĴps://www.verywellmind.com/how-to-boost-your-self-confidence-4163098)",
