      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nModern parenting advice often emphasizes the importance of helping children\nunderstand and regulate their emotions. Yet experts argue that parents’ own feelings are\njust as critical in shaping emotionally healthy families. In their new book Parents Have\nFeelings, Too, psychotherapist Hilary Jacobs Hendel and psychologist Dr. Juli Fraga stress\nthat mothers and fathers must learn to process their internal states before they can\neffectively guide their children. “Emotions affect us every day and often get us into\ntrouble, and they have huge repercussions for how we understand emotions for future\ngenerations,” Hendel explained. By acknowledging and working through personal\nemotions, parents create the conditions for secure attachment and resilient kids.\nCentral to Hendel and Fraga’s approach is a framework known as the Change Triangle,\nadapted from the AEDP model of psychotherapy. This tool encourages parents to name,\nvalidate, and navigate six core emotions—anger, sadness, fear, disgust, joy, and\nexcitement—rather than suppress them. According to Hendel, mental health means being\nable to experience the full range of these emotions with minimal defensiveness or\ninhibition. For example, anger often becomes destructive when avoided or denied,\nmanifesting as aggression, anxiety, or depression. By recognizing anger as an internal\nprocess and separating it from outward action, parents can release its energy in healthier\nways and model constructive emotional regulation for their children.\nThe book also challenges myths surrounding emotions, such as the belief that they can be\nconsciously controlled through toughness or willpower. Hendel explains that emotions\nare rapid survival mechanisms, originating in parts of the brain outside conscious awareness. Defenses—like overworking, shutting down, or playing video games\nexcessively—often develop to protect individuals from painful feelings but carry long-\nterm costs for relationships and health. Fraga points out that fathers as well as mothers\nexperience intense emotions, including shame and “dad rage,” yet men’s struggles are\nrarely validated. Recognizing these patterns helps both parents become more present and\nresponsive in family life.\nUltimately, the goal of emotional education for parents is not to eliminate negative\nfeelings but to move through them toward calm, compassion, and connection—the “four\nCs” at the heart of the Change Triangle. Techniques such as practicing gratitude, savoring\nmoments of joy, and reflecting on bodily sensations during emotional experiences can\ndeepen resilience and open doors for genuine communication with children. As Fraga\nemphasizes, it is never too late: the brain’s neuroplasticity allows parents to rewire\nemotional habits at any stage of life. By learning to work through their own emotions,\nparents not only enhance their personal well-being but also raise children who are better\nequipped to navigate the complex emotional landscape of adulthood.\nAdapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/09/25/health/parents-feelings-emotional-health-book-\nwellness\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
