      "sectionName": "Sử dụng Tiếng Anh (300đ)",
      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully. Implicit memory and explicit memory are two key types of long-term memory.\nInformation you remember unconsciously and effortlessly—like how to ride a bike or tie\nyour shoes—is stored as implicit memory. Meanwhile, information that you have to\nconsciously recall and think about, such as remembering your to-do list or a historical\ndate, is known as explicit memory. Both forms of memory play crucial roles in how we\nrecall information and interact with our surroundings. Understanding the difference\nbetween them helps explain how the brain organizes and retrieves knowledge.\nImplicit memory, sometimes called unconscious or automatic memory, refers to\ninformation that people don’t intentionally try to remember. It’s also known as\nnondeclarative memory because it cannot easily be expressed in words. These memories\nare procedural—focused on how to perform actions or skills that become second nature\nthrough repetition. For example, singing a familiar song, typing on a keyboard, or driving\na car all rely on implicit memory. Even after years without practice, most people can hop\non a bicycle and ride effortlessly because the memory of the task remains intact. Implicit\nmemory mainly involves the cerebellum and basal ganglia, which coordinate movement\nand habit formation. Although we’re not aware of these memories, they strongly influence\nour behaviors and knowledge of different tasks.\nBy contrast, explicit memory—sometimes called declarative memory—involves\nconscious, intentional recollection. When you try to recall what you studied for a test, the\nname of your childhood friend, or the capital of a country, you are using explicit memory.\nThis type of memory depends on the hippocampus and frontal lobes. Explicit memory\ncan be divided into two categories: episodic memory, which includes personal\nexperiences such as birthdays or vacations, and semantic memory, which stores facts,\nconcepts, and general knowledge. Remembering your phone number, identifying a\nfamous person, or recalling when your next appointment is are all examples of explicit\nrecall.\nWhile the two systems differ in awareness, they often work together in daily life. Implicit\nmemory lets you engage automatically in learned behaviors—like steering a car—while\nexplicit memory allows you to consciously remember directions or a new route. Research\nshows that factors such as stress, mood, and age can affect both types: high stress may\nimpair explicit recall but heighten emotional implicit memories, while aging tends to\nweaken explicit memory yet preserve implicit skills. Maintaining healthy habits like\nregular sleep, physical exercise, brain training, and balanced nutrition can help protect\nboth systems. Ultimately, implicit memory lets you act without thinking, while explicit\nmemory lets you think before acting—and together, they form the foundation of learning\nand daily functioning.\n(Adapted from:\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
