      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nThe familiar “chasing arrows” symbol — three curved triangles forming a loop —\nappears on almost every plastic package, giving the illusion of sustainability. Yet that\nsymbol doesn’t guarantee recyclability at all. Manufacturers can print it freely; its true\npurpose is to identify the type of plastic used. A “1” or “2” often means the material is\nwidely recyclable, while a “3” indicates PVC — rarely accepted in curbside programs.\nConfusion over this logo became so widespread that California recently banned its use on products that aren’t truly recyclable. The result is a system that asks individuals to\ninterpret a cryptic code, where the burden of understanding falls on the consumer rather\nthan the producer.\nEven for those who follow the rules, the results can be disheartening. Only an estimated\n9 percent of all plastics ever made have been recycled. The rest have been buried, burned,\nor scattered across the planet. The reasons are structural: every community operates\ndifferently, with local policies determining what can and cannot be accepted. Some towns\nhave suspended programs entirely, while others lack buyers for sorted plastic waste,\nleaving bales to pile up in warehouses. Even advanced optical scanners that identify\nplastics by shape or color often misread dark containers or thin films. What can be recycled\nis typically “downcycled” into lower-value materials like carpet fiber or polyester, while\nthe remainder is dumped or incinerated — releasing greenhouse gases and toxic fumes.\nThe scale of the problem keeps growing. Since 1960, global plastic production has\nincreased nearly one hundredfold, with more than half of all items designed for single\nuse. Plastics persist for centuries, fragmenting into microplastics found in rain, oceans,\nand even human blood. At the root of this surge is cheap oil and gas — the feedstock for\nmost plastics — which has made them profitable even as nations pledge to burn fewer\nfossil fuels. The paradox is striking: plastics can make cars lighter and packaging more\nenergy-efficient, yet their manufacture and disposal emit more greenhouse gases than the\nentire aviation industry.\nIt wasn’t always this way. Early plastics were celebrated as a sustainable substitute for\nivory and tortoiseshell, but by the mid-20th century, manufacturers embraced\ndisposability as a business model. When the mounting waste drew backlash, industry\nleaders promoted curbside recycling — shifting responsibility from producers to the\npublic. Decades later, the promise remains unfulfilled. As environmentalists note,\nrecycling alone cannot solve the plastic crisis. Without systemic change — reducing\nproduction, redesigning materials, and enforcing accountability — the arrows on the\nbottom of our bottles will continue to point in circles.\n(Adapted from: https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/04/21/climate/plastics-recycling-\ntrash-environment.html)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
