      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nIn southern Florida, a heated debate surrounds the fate of Florida Bay — a vast, shallow\nstretch of water between the Everglades and the Florida Keys. Once known for its clear\nwaters, lush seagrass beds, and thriving coral reefs, the bay has been suffering from\necological decline. Since the 1980s, coral cover has dropped by 40%, seagrass beds have\ndied back, and fish populations have dwindled. The U.S. government’s $8 billion plan to\nrestore natural freshwater flow through the Everglades aims to reverse the damage, but\nscientists disagree on whether this intervention will heal or further harm the ecosystem.\nMarine ecologist Joseph Zieman argues that a lack of freshwater is the bay’s core problem.\nHe blames a century of Everglades drainage, which reduced freshwater inflows and\nincreased salinity. The resulting hypersaline conditions, he says, killed seagrasses; their\ndecay released nutrients that triggered algae blooms, turning the bay’s waters cloudy and\nblocking sunlight — a destructive cycle of death and turbidity. In his view, restoring\nfreshwater flow will rebalance salinity and revive the bay’s ecology.\nHowever, Brian Lapointe of the Harbour Branch Oceanographic Institution disputes this\nexplanation. He contends that seagrasses can tolerate current salinity levels and that\nprevious increases in freshwater runoff during the 1990s worsened conditions. Nutrient-\nrich water from fertilized farmland, he says, flooded the bay with nitrogen, promoting\nmassive plankton blooms and further seagrass die-offs. Because coral reefs thrive only in\nnutrient-poor environments, Lapointe warns that additional freshwater carrying nitrogen\ncould devastate the Keys’ reef system. Bill Kruczynski of the Environmental Protection\nAgency adds another factor: sewage pollution from thousands of coastal cesspits may also\nbe fueling nutrient overloads.\nThe conflicting theories make predicting the outcome of the Everglades restoration\ndifficult. If high salinity is the true cause, more freshwater will help; if nitrogen pollution\nis to blame, the plan could worsen the problem. Solutions such as building wetlands to\nfilter farm runoff or replacing cesspits with proper sewerage systems could cost hundreds\nof millions of dollars. Meanwhile, the stakes are high: the Florida Keys’ $2.5 billion\ntourism industry — dependent on coral reefs, fishing, and clear waters — could face\neconomic disaster if restoration efforts backfire. Ultimately, scientists caution that\n“returning” the bay to its pre-drainage state may be impossible, since no one knows\nexactly what that natural balance once was.\n(Adapted from: https://mini-ielts.com/1381/reading/pollution-in-the-bay)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
