      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nSoutheast Asia is at a decisive turning point in its energy future. Projected to account for\n25% of global energy demand growth by 2035, the region must balance rising\nconsumption with intensifying climate risks and security concerns. Governments and\ninstitutions such as the ASEAN Center for Energy (ACE) are setting ambitious net-zero\ntargets—eight member states have already committed—but progress has been uneven.\nRenewables currently make up just 15.6% of the energy mix, falling short of the 23% target\nby 2025. Financing gaps, weak grid infrastructure, and fragmented policies remain\nobstacles. Recognizing these challenges, Malaysia’s Sarawak Energy is hosting the fourth\nSustainable and Renewable Energy Forum (SAREF 4.0) in September 2025, convening\nregional and global stakeholders under the theme “Partnerships for Our Goals” to\nadvance collective solutions. Clean energy is expected to meet about one-third of the projected growth in demand, with\nhydropower continuing to play a leading role. Malaysia aims to raise its renewable\ncapacity to 40% by 2035, supported by Sarawak Energy’s plan to deliver 15 GW of\ncapacity, at least 60% of which will come from renewables. The company is scaling up\nboth hydro and solar, including a 50-MW floating solar farm at Batang Ai Hydroelectric\nPlant and plans for 1.5 GW of solar capacity by 2030. These projects underscore the need\nfor diversification in building a more secure and flexible regional grid that can integrate\nintermittent energy sources while sustaining industrial growth.\nTechnological innovation is central to this transformation. Sarawak Energy is deploying\nsmart grid systems, predictive maintenance, AI-enabled monitoring, and advanced\nmetering infrastructure (AMI) to modernize its network. Malaysia’s first large-scale\nbattery energy storage system (BESS), launched in 2024 at Sejingkat Power Plant, is\nstrengthening grid resilience while enabling greater integration of variable renewables\nlike solar. Alongside these advances, Sarawak is investing in nature-based solutions such\nas mangrove restoration and peatland conservation to bolster carbon sequestration, and\nexperimenting with biomass and sustainable aviation fuels derived from agricultural\nwaste and microalgae. These efforts highlight how technology and ecological stewardship\ncan complement one another in building energy-smart ecosystems.\nRegional cooperation will be vital to sustaining momentum. Sarawak’s Post COVID-19\nDevelopment Strategy 2030 positions clean energy as a driver of economic transformation,\nwhile initiatives such as the ASEAN Power Grid (APG) envision interconnected\ntransmission across all ten member states by 2045. Interconnections like the Borneo Grid,\nalready linking Sarawak to West Kalimantan, are early steps toward a regional clean\nenergy market. The stakes are high: the International Energy Agency estimates that\nASEAN’s transition could create 66 million jobs and add $5.3 trillion to the economy by\n2050. With forums like SAREF 4.0 translating ambition into concrete partnerships,\nSoutheast Asia has the opportunity not only to meet its own energy needs but also to\nestablish itself as a leader in the global low-carbon transition.\nAdapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/sponsor/edition/sarawak-energy/powering-a-clean-\nfuture?utm_medium=cnn&utm_source=bcp&utm_campaign=sarawakenergy\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
