      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nFor centuries, medicine has wrestled with the mystery of healing—whether recovery\nstems from the treatment itself or from the patient’s belief in it. Geoff Watts opens by\ndescribing how easy it would be to invent a new “alternative therapy”: offer warmth,\ntouch, reassurance, and an air of mystical wisdom, and patients will often feel better.\nMany such treatments succeed not because of their medical content but because they\nharness the human mind’s capacity to trigger healing. This phenomenon, known as the\nplacebo effect, explains why patients sometimes improve even when receiving inactive or\nsymbolic remedies.\nWatts notes that the placebo effect can make even fraudulent or unscientific methods\nappear effective. Since many illnesses naturally improve over time, any coincidental\nrecovery reinforces a therapist’s reputation. Yet psychology also plays a real role—belief,\nexpectation, and trust activate biological responses that conventional medicine still\nstruggles to explain. Both mainstream and complementary practitioners unknowingly\nrely on this force, though alternative medicine often excels at creating the right emotional\nand relational conditions for it to work. Their longer sessions, physical contact, and\nempathetic communication can mobilize a patient’s confidence and, with it, measurable\nphysiological changes.\nScientific research into how placebos work has focused largely on pain control. Studies\nreveal that believing in a treatment can stimulate the release of endorphins, the body’s\nnatural painkillers. Experiments by Fabrizio Benedetti in Turin provided direct proof:\nwhen patients were unknowingly given saline instead of morphine, their pain still\neased—until another drug, naloxone, blocked the endorphins and the relief vanished.\nAlthough scientists do not yet understand how belief triggers such chemical reactions,\nevidence shows that visual and contextual cues matter greatly. Red pills, for example,\nseem to work better for pain than blue ones; branded tablets outperform identical\ngenerics. Even a doctor’s enthusiasm can determine how well a medicine appears to work. Watts concludes that warmth, empathy, and human connection are as vital to healing as\nthe drugs themselves. The most successful practitioners—whether conventional or\nalternative—are those who combine scientific competence with emotional intelligence.\nFar from being mere trickery, the placebo effect reveals the extraordinary interplay\nbetween mind and body. Recognizing and ethically applying this power could transform\nthe way medicine is practiced: rather than dismissing belief as illusion, doctors should see\nit as a natural component of recovery. In the end, the “power of nothing” is not empty at\nall—it is the power of expectation, trust, and the human capacity to heal itself.\n(Adapted from: https://mini-ielts.com/1358/reading/the-power-of-nothing)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
