      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nHundreds of scientists around the world are launching an unprecedented conservation\ncampaign — to save microbes. Though invisible to the naked eye, these microscopic\norganisms are vital to every ecosystem on Earth. “We need them in order to help conserve\nthe pandas, the rainforests, the whales, and everything else in between,” said Jack Gilbert,\na microbiologist at the University of California, San Diego. For decades, conservation has\nfocused mainly on animals and plants. The International Union for Conservation of\nNature (IUCN), which manages the global Red List of endangered species, has recently\nshifted its attention to fungi and now to bacteria and other microbes. Researchers estimate\nthere may be as many as 100 billion microbial species—making this effort perhaps the\nmost daunting conservation challenge in history.\nMicrobes are the foundation of Earth’s biodiversity. They live in soil, oceans, clouds, and\neven inside living organisms. Yet many microbial habitats are being destroyed by\ndeforestation, urban expansion, and intensive agriculture. Soil alone may contain half of\nall microbial species, but as forests are cut down and farmlands spread, much of that\nhidden diversity is lost forever. To combat this, scientists have created the Microbial Conservation Specialist Group, which aims to identify global hot spots of microbial\ndiversity, collect samples, and store them in protected vaults. “The way forward is to\npreserve the ecosystem so you can preserve the microorganisms, and the microorganisms\ncan in turn improve the ecosystem in a positive loop,” said microbiologist Raquel Peixoto,\nthe group’s co-chair.\nMicrobial conservation also offers direct benefits to humans. Coral reefs, for instance,\ndepend on microbial communities that protect corals from disease and help them obtain\nnutrients. As oceans warm and become more polluted, beneficial microbes decline while\npathogens thrive. Dr. Peixoto’s experiments using probiotic microbes have shown early\nsuccess in restoring coral health, suggesting that preserving microbial balance may help\nprotect entire marine ecosystems. Likewise, in deserts, microbial crusts remove carbon\ndioxide from the atmosphere, helping slow climate change. “Five hundred acres of that\ndesert will sequester a lot more carbon than one acre will,” Dr. Gilbert noted.\nProtecting microbes could also transform agriculture. Instead of relying on costly\nchemical fertilizers, farmers could cultivate soil bacteria that naturally draw nitrogen from\nthe air, saving money and reducing environmental damage. Such examples highlight how\nmicrobial conservation supports both ecological stability and human prosperity. As Dr.\nPeixoto emphasized, microbes are not just passive background life forms — they are active\nengineers of the planet’s health. Preserving them means safeguarding the living systems\nthat sustain us all. “The more microbes we save,” she said, “the more they can help save\nus.”\n(Adapted from: https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/17/science/microbes-endangered-\npreservation.html)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
      "question": "It is not mentioned in paragraph 2 that scientists will ________.",
