      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nResearchers have developed a self-healing concrete capable of automatically sealing small\ncracks, allowing building walls to “heal” themselves much like human skin regenerates\nafter a cut or scrape. The innovation relies on a bacterial additive that triggers the\nformation of calcium carbonate, a white mineral often seen as limescale in sinks and\nshowers. This compound, known for its durability and stability, has been associated with\nthe self-healing properties found in ancient structures such as the Pantheon and the\nColosseum. “Calcium carbonate forms naturally from calcium alone, but bacteria\naccelerate this transformation,” explains Alejandro Montesinos, head of the\nDecarbonization, Climate Change, and Circular Economy Research Group at Tec de\nMonterrey. The key player in this process is the bacterium Bacillus subtilis, which feeds on carbon\ndioxide (CO₂) and produces calcium carbonate through biomineralization. In the presence\nof moisture, the bacteria use nutrients such as calcium and magnesium, capturing CO₂\nand combining these elements to form mineral deposits that gradually turn into rock. As\na result, the bacteria effectively seal cracks in concrete as part of their life cycle. Before\ndying, the organisms release spores that remain dormant until conditions become\nfavorable again—ensuring that the self-healing process can restart years later. This\ntechnique not only strengthens materials but also offers a sustainable approach to\nreducing emissions from the cement industry, which generates roughly 2.3 billion tons of\nCO₂ annually, or about 7 percent of global emissions.\nA major challenge is ensuring that the bacteria can survive in concrete’s highly alkaline\nenvironment. Standard strains die quickly due to its extreme pH. To solve this, researchers\nembed bacterial spores in a protective matrix within the concrete mix. When a crack forms,\nthe spores are exposed, germinate, and begin producing calcium carbonate in the presence\nof water and nutrients. In laboratory tests, narrow cracks measuring one to two\nmillimeters wide healed completely within 15 to 30 days. The next stage of research\ninvolves adapting the process for real-world construction under different humidity and\nCO₂ levels.\nAlthough promising, this self-healing concrete is intended primarily for small fissures\nrather than large structural fractures. “It’s not suitable for load-bearing walls or heavy-\nsupport structures,” notes Montesinos. The bacteria can function in both indoor and\noutdoor settings but cannot survive extreme heat above 100 °C. Still, their spores may\nremain viable for decades, potentially reactivating when exposed to moisture. Montesinos\nenvisions this material as a finishing layer, similar to plaster, that could enhance durability\nwhile supporting circular-economy principles. Ultimately, this research reframes CO₂ not\nas waste but as a valuable resource—one that can help build structures capable of\nrepairing themselves and reducing the environmental impact of modern construction.\n(Adapted from: https://tecscience.tec.mx/en/biotechnology/self-healing-concrete/)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
