      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nTekedra Mawakana, the co-CEO of Waymo, has long recognized that the story of self-\ndriving cars is filled with lofty promises but marked by difficult realities. Speaking at\nTechCrunch Disrupt 2025, she emphasized that scaling autonomous vehicle services is not\nonly a matter of advanced technology, but also one of safety, public trust, and regulatory\ncompliance. Robotaxis cannot be deployed at massive scale as long as they rely on safety\ndrivers or remote interventions in unpredictable situations. For her, the path forward\nrequires patience, transparency, and incremental expansion rather than marketing-driven\noptimism.\nOver the years, Waymo has faced pressure from competitors and media scrutiny as\ncrashes, glitches, and overhyped demonstrations shaped public perception. Mawakana\nstressed that the tech industry must be more responsible in how it communicates\nprogress—drawing a clear line between what is proven and what remains experimental.\nFlashy demos can mislead the public into believing the technology is fully ready, when\nin reality it performs reliably only under controlled conditions. She warned that inflated\npredictions without solid evidence could damage long-term trust, not just in Waymo but\nin the entire autonomous vehicle sector.\nMawakana underscored that moving from limited pilots to large-scale deployment\nrequires far more than sophisticated algorithms. It involves robust data collection,\nmonitoring systems, maintenance infrastructure, and seamless human intervention when\nnecessary. Every city presents unique traffic behaviors, weather patterns, and\ninfrastructure challenges. A system must be resilient to heavy rain, snow, or unpredictable\npedestrian actions, and able to adapt to diverse regulatory frameworks. According to\nMawakana, there is no “one-size-fits-all” solution for self-driving technology, which is\nwhy deployment will always require local tailoring and cautious testing.\nLooking ahead, Mawakana takes a pragmatic view of autonomy. The goal is not an\nimmediate leap to driverless vehicles everywhere, but a gradual reduction of human\ninvolvement as the technology proves itself. She revealed that Waymo is working on\nsystems that can recover gracefully from errors or bring the vehicle to a safe stop if needed.\nAt the same time, she called on investors, regulators, and the public to ask tougher\nquestions: What evidence shows these vehicles are truly safer than human drivers? How\nmany incidents occur per million miles? When will safety drivers no longer be required?\nFor Mawakana, these are the questions that must guide the industry if autonomous\nmobility is to evolve from futuristic ideal to sustainable reality.\n(Adapted from: https://techcrunch.com)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
