      "sectionName": "Sử dụng Tiếng Anh (300đ)",
      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nA massive bushfire north of Sydney merged from several smaller blazes on December 6,\ncreating what firefighters now call a “mega fire.” Fueled by fierce winds and bone-dry\nconditions, the blaze has blanketed Australia’s largest city in thick, hazardous smoke that\nhas turned the sky a deep orange and forced commuters to wear masks. Officials warn\nthat it could take weeks to bring the fire under control.\nSo far, the Gospers Mountain fire alone has scorched more than 3,000 square kilometers,\nmaking it larger than California’s biggest blaze on record — the Mendocino Complex Fire\nof 2018. Across the state of New South Wales, more than 21,000 square kilometers of land\nhave burned, an area roughly equal to 94 percent of New Jersey’s size. The infernos have\nkilled at least four people and destroyed more than 680 homes since early November.\nDozens of smaller fires are still active, stretching firefighters to their limits and threatening\nrural towns already suffering from drought.\nAustralia’s summer wildfire season normally peaks in January or February, but this year\nflames arrived months earlier. Scientists and government agencies partly blame a\nwarming climate and a three-year drought that has left the landscape tinder-dry. Satellite\nmaps show vast zones of extremely dry bushland across New South Wales and southern\nQueensland. Root-zone soil moisture in many regions has fallen to record lows, meaning\nthat even the smallest spark — from lightning, machinery, or a campfire — can ignite\ncatastrophic blazes that spread uncontrollably through forest and farmland alike.\nThe smoke crisis has triggered public outrage and reignited debate over Australia’s\nclimate policies. Farmers say the drought has devastated crops and livestock, while city\ndwellers have endured weeks of choking air. Environmental groups and protesters are\ndemanding stronger national action to curb greenhouse-gas emissions and reduce fire\nrisks. Yet amid the destruction, Australians continue to show resilience: communities are\ndonating supplies, volunteers are supporting exhausted fire crews, and scientists are\nracing to model how extreme weather may shape the country’s future fire seasons. The\n“mega fire” around Sydney, experts warn, is not an isolated event — it may be a preview\nof the new normal in a warming world.\n(Adapted from: https://www.reuters.com/graphics/AUSTRALIA-BUSHFIRES-\nSIZE%20/0100B4QG2FG/)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
