      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nScientists have achieved a breakthrough that could reshape the future of reproductive\nmedicine: creating functional human eggs from skin cells. The proof-of-concept study,\npublished in Nature Communications, involved transferring the nucleus of an ordinary\nskin cell into a donor egg whose nucleus had been removed. This produced 82\nreprogrammed oocytes, which could then undergo fertilization in the laboratory.\nAccording to Dr. Paula Amato of Oregon Health & Science University, one of the study’s\nauthors, the technique could eventually allow older women or cancer survivors who lost fertility to have genetically related children. The approach may even give same-sex male\ncouples the opportunity to have offspring who share DNA with both partners.\nTo make this possible, researchers developed a process they call “mitomeiosis”, designed\nto mimic natural cell division so that eggs end up with the correct number of\nchromosomes. Ordinary human cells contain 46 chromosomes, while sex cells such as\neggs and sperm carry only 23. Ensuring this reduction was crucial, but the results were\nmixed: fewer than 9% of the engineered eggs reached the blastocyst stage, the point at\nwhich embryos are typically transferred during IVF. Moreover, all of the embryos were\nchromosomally abnormal, either with too many chromosomes or with mismatched pairs,\nmeaning they would not be expected to develop into healthy babies.\nDespite these setbacks, experts view the study as an important step forward. Dr.\nShoukhrat Mitalipov, senior author and director of the OHSU Center for Embryonic Cell\nand Gene Therapy, emphasized that natural reproduction is itself inefficient, with only a\nthird of embryos developing to blastocyst. Other researchers, like UCLA’s Amander\nClark, called the work “an important beginning” but stressed that major technical\nimprovements are needed before it could be applied clinically. Because the method is\nbased on somatic cell nuclear transfer, the same technique that produced Dolly the sheep\nin 1997, ethical and regulatory hurdles will also be high. Human cloning remains\nprohibited in many countries, and any procedure linked to that technology is likely to face\nintense scrutiny.\nEven so, the potential implications are profound. Millions of women worldwide suffer\nfrom primary ovarian insufficiency or infertility that cannot be treated through\nconventional IVF. For them, transformative new technologies will be essential if they wish\nto conceive. Experts such as Professor Ying Cheong of the University of Southampton\nargue that while clinical use may be a decade away, the study could one day revolutionize\nhow doctors approach infertility and miscarriage. By demonstrating that DNA from\nordinary skin cells can be reprogrammed into fertilizable eggs, scientists have taken a bold\nfirst step toward expanding the possibilities of family building in ways previously\nthought impossible.\n(Adapted from: https://edition.cnn.com/2025/09/30/science/human-skin-cells-egg-infertility)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
