      "passage": "Questions 53-60: Read the passage carefully.\nIn recent months, social media has been filled with videos in which people “speak” to the\nperson they were ten years ago. Using old photographs, editing tools, and split-screen\neffects, they create an imaginary conversation between their younger and present selves.\nAt first glance, the trend may seem sentimental or purely entertaining. Yet many psychologists would argue that it reflects something deeper: humans need to make sense\nof personal change over time and to see one’s life as a meaningful story rather than a series\nof disconnected events.\nOne reason this trend resonates so widely is that it allows people to look at the past from\na safer emotional distance. Instead of reliving an old disappointment from inside the\nconfusion of that moment, they can now examine it with greater maturity and perspective.\nA memory that once caused embarrassment may appear less like evidence of failure and\nmore like proof of effort, endurance, or growth. This shift can be especially meaningful\nfor people who have long struggled with self-doubt, because it helps them see that their\npresent abilities were not the result of luck alone, but of gradual learning through\nexperience.\nThe practice also challenges the idea that life should unfold according to a fixed timetable.\nMany people grow up believing that by a certain age they should already have achieved\ncareer success, financial stability, or emotional certainty. Social media often strengthens\nthis pressure by presenting polished highlights rather than the hidden struggles behind\nthem. Looking back over a period of ten years, however, often reveals that development\nis rarely smooth or predictable. Progress may be slow, uneven, and invisible for long\nstretches, yet that does not make it unreal. What maĴers is not whether someone has\nfollowed a universal schedule, but whether they have continued moving forward in their\nown way.\nAnother important benefit of this kind of reflection is that it can deepen self-compassion.\nWhen people imagine speaking to their younger selves, they are often less harsh than they\nwould be in ordinary self-criticism. They may recognize that the person they once were\nwas doing the best they could with limited confidence, knowledge, or support. Instead of\ncondemning past mistakes, they may begin to understand them as natural parts of\ngrowing up. This kinder interpretation does not excuse poor decisions, but it does place\nthem in a broader human context, making it easier to learn from them without becoming\ntrapped in shame.\nFor that reason, the value of this trend lies not in nostalgia alone, but in the insight it can\nproduce. Looking back should not be an exercise in regret or self-punishment. Rather, it\ncan help people integrate the past into a fuller understanding of who they are now. Every\nstage of life, including the awkward or painful ones, contributes something to personal\nidentity. By acknowledging that continuity, individuals may become more resilient, more\npatient with themselves, and more hopeful about the future. In the end, speaking to one’s\nyounger self is really another way of learning how to speak more wisely to the person one\nis today.\n(Adapted from: greater good and various sources)",
