      "section": "english",
      "sectionName": "Sử dụng Tiếng Anh (300đ)",
      "passage": "Questions: Read the passage carefully.\nSome of the most creative minds in history — from Pablo Picasso and J.K. Rowling to Jean-\nPaul Sartre and Bob Dylan — have found inspiration not in private studios or silent offices,\nbut in bustling cafés. These public spaces, filled with conversation, clinking cups, and\nbackground music, seem to spark imagination in ways that home or office environments\nrarely can. During the pandemic, as millions of people began working remotely, many\ndiscovered they missed the energy of coffee shops — even though they had been working “alone” there too. Why does sitting among strangers somehow make us think more\ncreatively?\nPsychologists suggest that coffee shops strike a unique balance between focus and\ndistraction. A landmark 2012 study published in the Journal of Consumer Research\nshowed that a moderate level of background noise — similar to that in a café — can boost\ncreative thinking. The subtle hum of activity slightly diverts attention from the task at\nhand, encouraging abstract thought and original ideas. Later studies found a similar\n“Goldilocks effect” known as stochastic resonance, where just the right amount of sensory\nnoise sharpens perception and decision-making. Add to that the so-called “coffee shop\neffect”, where seeing others absorbed in their own work acts as silent motivation — much\nlike exercising harder at a gym when others are pushing themselves nearby.\nBeyond sound and social presence, visual variety also stimulates the brain. Working in\nthe same home office or cubicle day after day can dull creativity, while the ever-changing\nenvironment of a café — the movement of people, the shifting daylight, the smell of\nroasted beans — provides subtle sensory refreshment. Architectural researcher Korydon\nSmith argues that these “micro-stimuli” unconsciously nudge our brains to think\ndifferently. Even décor variety plays a role: unlike the predictable design of offices, cafés\noffer spontaneous visual surprises that keep our minds alert and flexible.\nFinally, the informality of cafés may help both individuals and groups think more freely.\nUnlike scheduled meetings or rigid office settings, conversations over coffee unfold\nnaturally, often leading to unexpected ideas. Architects note that modern campuses and\noffices now borrow this coffee-shop design — open seating, natural light, casual gathering\nzones — to encourage collaboration and creativity. Not every public space works equally\nwell, of course; bars, for instance, are usually too noisy for productive thought. Yet despite\nremote work becoming more common, researchers predict that many will keep returning\nto cafés. After all, the mix of mild noise, human energy, and sensory richness offers a\ncreative boost that even the best home office can’t quite match — and yes, the coffee helps\ntoo.\n(Adapted from: https://www.bbc.com/worklife/article/20210114-why-youre-more-creative-in-\ncoffee-shops)\nChoose an option (A, B, C, or D) that best answers each question.",
